{"term":"日你妈","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"rì nǐ mā","category":"insults","subcategory":"maternal_sexual_insult","concept":"motherfucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":98,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'fuck your mother'; an extremely offensive maternal sexual insult expressing maximum contempt or rage, equivalent in force to English 'fuck your mother'.","english_term":"fuck your mother","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"日 (rì) is a crude verb meaning 'to fuck', functionally synonymous with 操 (cào) but more common in northern dialects. Among the most severe Mandarin insults alongside 操你妈."}
{"term":"操你全家","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"cào nǐ quánjiā","category":"insults","subcategory":"family_sexual_insult","concept":"whole_family_sexual_insult","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"slang_crude","crude":99,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'fuck your entire family'; a maximalist sexual insult extending condemnation beyond the mother to every family member, weaponizing collective family honor as the target.","english_term":"fuck your whole family","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Escalation of 操你妈; the extension to 全家 (entire family) intensifies severity by implicating collective honor. In Chinese culture, where family reputation is a central social currency, the broadening is a deliberate escalation strategy absent in English idiom."}
{"term":"婊子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"biǎozi","category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":94,"recognition_pct_peak":94,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A woman regarded as sexually promiscuous or morally loose; historically denoted a prostitute, now functions as a general pejorative for a contemptible or 'slutty' woman.","english_term":"whore / bitch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Historically 婊子 specifically meant prostitute; in modern usage it has broadened to a general female insult implying sexual availability and low character, parallel to the semantic drift of English 'bitch' and 'whore'."}
{"term":"臭婊子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"chòu biǎozi","category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":95,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'stinking whore/bitch'; 臭 (stinking/smelly) prefixed to 婊子 adds olfactory disgust, intensifying the sexual and moral condemnation.","english_term":"dirty whore / stinking bitch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"臭 (chòu, stinking) is a highly productive Mandarin intensifier for insults; it adds visceral disgust to any base insult. The olfactory component may carry an implicit reference to genital odor as part of the slur's mechanism."}
{"term":"荡妇","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"dàngfù","category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"sexual_promiscuity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":77,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A wanton or promiscuous woman; 荡 implies moral dissolution and unrestrained behavior, 妇 means woman; used as an insult for a sexually licentious female.","english_term":"slut / wanton woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Slightly more literary than 婊子; 荡 (dàng) literally means 'to sway or drift' and by extension moral looseness. Appears in classical literature and contemporary usage alike; slightly less raw than 婊子 because of its literary register."}
{"term":"骚货","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"sāohuò","category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"promiscuous_person_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":91,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'horny/lewd goods/merchandise'; degrades a sexually provocative or available woman by reducing her to a commodity, combining lewdness (骚) with the dehumanizing 货 (goods).","english_term":"horny slut / lewd piece","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"骚 (sāo) means smelly, lewd, or sexually provocative; 货 (huò, goods/merchandise) dehumanizes by commodifying the target. Can occasionally be deployed flirtatiously between intimate partners, parallel to reclaimed use of 'slut' in English."}
{"term":"贱人","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jiànrén","category":"insults","subcategory":"contemptible_person","concept":"bitch_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":83,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'cheap/lowly person'; a general insult for a contemptible, low-character individual, disproportionately directed at women with implied sexual looseness.","english_term":"bitch / low-life","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"贱 (jiàn) means cheap, base, or of low social standing — originally a caste marker. While etymologically gender-neutral, it is overwhelmingly used against women in practice. Phrase 贱人就是矫情 ('bitches are just dramatic') from a popular TV drama greatly amplified its circulation post-2011."}
{"term":"贱货","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jiànhuò","category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":88,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'cheap goods/merchandise'; reduces a woman to a low-value sexual commodity, combining class contempt (贱, cheap) with the dehumanizing objectification of 货 (goods).","english_term":"cheap slut / piece of trash","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More explicitly gendered and sexualizing than 贱人 because 货 frames the target as an object to be used. Combines economic contempt (low price = low worth) with sexual condemnation. Frequently appears in online misogynistic discourse."}
{"term":"臭逼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"chòu bī","category":"insults","subcategory":"vulva_derived_insult","concept":"cunt_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":95,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN","definition":"Literally 'stinking cunt'; a maximally offensive insult compounding the crude vagina term 逼 (bī) with 臭 (stinking) to produce visceral sexual and olfactory contempt.","english_term":"stinking cunt","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"逼 (bī) is the primary crude Mandarin term for the vagina, parallel in force to English 'cunt'. 臭 adds olfactory disgust. Somewhat more regionally concentrated in northern China; less common than 傻逼 but more viscerally offensive."}
{"term":"傻逼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"shǎ bī","category":"insults","subcategory":"vulva_derived_insult","concept":"cunt_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":92,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'stupid cunt'; the most widely used 逼-compound insult in Mandarin, calling someone a complete idiot with maximum vulgarity.","english_term":"stupid cunt / fucking idiot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The most common and recognizable 逼-compound. 傻 (shǎ) means stupid or foolish. Routinely censored online as SB or replaced with 沙比 / 傻B. Ubiquitous in mainland Chinese internet culture and casual speech; the 逼-compound idiom functions more like a profanity intensifier than a literal anatomical reference."}
{"term":"牛逼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"niú bī","category":"insults","subcategory":"vulgar_positive_exclamation","concept":"vulgar_excellence_exclamation","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"slang_crude","crude":60,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'cow/bull cunt'; paradoxically a strongly positive exclamation meaning 'awesome, badass, or impressively capable'; one of the most common Mandarin positive intensifiers despite its anatomical etymology.","english_term":"badass / awesome","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Functions exclusively as a positive intensifier in modern use, completely divorced from its anatomical etymology — a semantic bleaching without parallel in English. Often written as NB or 牛B in filtered contexts. The bull (牛) may evoke superior power rather than the species. Contrast with 装逼 (pretending to be impressive) and 屌丝 (a loser); 牛逼 is the positive pole of the same status-register vocabulary system."}
{"term":"屌丝","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"diǎosī","category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"genitalia_derived_loser_term","concept":"incel","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":2013,"regional":"CN","definition":"Literally 'dick hair/wire'; an internet-era self-deprecating term for a young man of low income, low social status, and poor romantic prospects — a sardonic loser identity adopted as ironic community solidarity.","english_term":"loser / incel","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Coined around 2010–2012 on Chinese forums, peaked 2013. Unlike English 'incel,' 屌丝 foregrounds class and economic failure as prominently as sexual failure, carries self-deprecating humor rather than explicit resentment, and was widely adopted as a collective identity by young Chinese men. Contrasted with 高富帅 (tall, rich, handsome). Still recognized but slightly dated."}
{"term":"王八蛋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"wángbā dàn","category":"insults","subcategory":"general_contempt","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'bastard egg of a cuckold-tortoise'; a strong general-purpose insult combining the classical cuckold-turtle symbol (王八) with 蛋 (egg/offspring) to imply corrupt and shameful lineage.","english_term":"bastard / son of a bitch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"王八 (wángbā) refers to the soft-shelled turtle, a classical Chinese symbol of cuckoldry — its association with being cheated on dates to imperial-era literary usage. 蛋 means both egg and offspring, implying the target is the product of a cuckolded lineage. Widely used in speech and film."}
{"term":"混蛋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"hùndàn","category":"insults","subcategory":"general_contempt","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":72,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'mixed egg'; a common, broadly applicable insult for a scoundrel or contemptible person, approximately equivalent to English 'bastard' or 'asshole'.","english_term":"bastard / jerk","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Less severe and more versatile than 王八蛋. 混 (hùn) means mixed, muddled, or corrupt, implying unclear or disreputable origins. Very common in everyday Mandarin speech, novels, and film — one of the default general-contempt insults."}
{"term":"杂种","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"zázhǒng","category":"insults","subcategory":"mixed_breed_insult","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":88,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'mixed breed/mongrel'; attacks lineage and character simultaneously, with connotations of illegitimate or racially mixed birth in addition to moral contemptibility.","english_term":"bastard / mongrel","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Carries potential racial connotations absent from most English 'bastard' usages; in some contexts implies ethnic mixing as a source of shame, making it more loaded than 混蛋. Used both to imply illegitimacy and as a generalized contempt insult; the biological breeding metaphor amplifies dehumanization."}
{"term":"王八","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"wángbā","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"cuckoldry_symbol","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":91,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'soft-shelled turtle'; the classical Chinese pejorative for a cuckold, with the turtle serving as an entrenched cultural symbol for a man whose wife is unfaithful; also a general contempt insult.","english_term":"cuckold / bastard","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The turtle-cuckold association is deeply embedded in Chinese cultural history, possibly originating in the Turtle's habit of hiding its head (metaphor for a husband blind to his wife's infidelity) or in Yuan-dynasty laws requiring certain groups to wear green headwear. 王八 is also sometimes parsed as a euphemism of 忘八 ('forgetting the eight Confucian virtues')."}
{"term":"绿帽子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"lǜ màozi","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"cuckoldry_symbol","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'green hat'; a culturally specific Chinese idiom meaning being cuckolded — i.e., having a spouse who commits adultery; wearing a green hat symbolizes the husband's shame.","english_term":"cuckold (symbolic)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Distinctly Chinese cultural symbol with no direct English equivalent idiom (European tradition uses 'wearing the horns'). Origin theories include Yuan-dynasty sumptuary laws forcing certain occupational groups (including relatives of sex workers) to wear green headwear. Widely recognized across all Mandarin-speaking regions and generations."}
{"term":"戴绿帽","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"dài lǜ mào","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"cuckoldry_symbol","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'to wear a green hat'; the verbal form of the cuckoldry idiom, meaning to be cuckolded (passive) or to cuckold someone (active/causative construction).","english_term":"to be cuckolded","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Verbal form of 绿帽子; 戴 (dài) means to wear on the head. Both active (给他戴绿帽, 'to put a green hat on him' = to cuckold him) and passive constructions are common. The verb form is frequently used in discussing infidelity in popular media and internet discourse."}
{"term":"缩卵","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"suō luǎn","category":"insults","subcategory":"cowardice_genitalia_metaphor","concept":"phallic_inadequacy_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":76,"recognition_pct_peak":76,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN","definition":"Literally 'shrinking balls/eggs'; an insult meaning to be cowardly or to back down from a challenge, using testicular retraction as the somatic metaphor for fear.","english_term":"chickening out / shrinking balls","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"卵 (luǎn) functions ambiguously as both 'eggs' and 'testicles' in crude speech. The physical image of testicular retraction from cold or fear provides the biological grounding for the cowardice metaphor. More common in northern Chinese dialects and less widely used than 没卵子."}
{"term":"没卵子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"méi luǎnzi","category":"insults","subcategory":"cowardice_genitalia_metaphor","concept":"phallic_inadequacy_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":81,"recognition_pct_peak":81,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'has no balls/eggs'; calls someone a coward by alleging the absence of testicles, directly parallel in structure and meaning to English 'no balls'.","english_term":"no balls / spineless","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"卵子 (luǎnzi) here uses the egg/ovum character for testicles in crude speech. Structurally identical to English 'no balls.' Applied to people of any gender despite the male anatomical reference; the testicle-as-courage metaphor is cross-culturally consistent."}
{"term":"装逼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"zhuāng bī","category":"insults","subcategory":"pretentious_posturing","concept":"douchebag_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":83,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":94,"recognition_pct_peak":94,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN","definition":"Literally 'to pretend/install cunt'; to put on airs, perform false superiority, or engage in status-seeking posturing — acting impressive when one is not.","english_term":"showing off / posing / being a douchebag","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"装 (zhuāng) means to install, pretend, or feign; 逼 adds vulgarity. The described behavior is performative status-seeking or pretentiousness. Central concept in Chinese internet cultural vocabulary, contrasted with 牛逼 (genuinely impressive) — 装逼 is fake 牛逼."}
{"term":"土逼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"tǔ bī","category":"insults","subcategory":"provincial_uncultured_insult","concept":"provincial_uncultured_insult","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"slang_crude","crude":85,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":74,"recognition_pct_peak":74,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN","definition":"Literally 'dirt/earth cunt'; an insult for someone unsophisticated, uncultured, or provincial — a bumpkin — with the crude 逼 (bī) amplifying class-based contempt to maximum vulgarity.","english_term":"uncultured hick / country bumpkin","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"土 (tǔ) means earth/soil and by extension: rustic, backward, unfashionable, or lacking urban cultural capital. The concept is more specific than English 'hick' because it targets the precise urban–rural cultural capital gap produced by China's rapid urbanization; a 土逼 specifically lacks the consumption markers and social codes of the urban middle class."}
{"term":"狗逼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gǒu bī","category":"insults","subcategory":"vulva_derived_insult","concept":"cunt_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":91,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":77,"recognition_pct_peak":77,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN","definition":"Literally 'dog cunt'; a maximally offensive compound insult combining 狗 (dog — a low-status animal in Chinese culture) with the crude vagina term 逼 to express deep contempt.","english_term":"filthy cunt / dog cunt","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Dogs carry stronger negative cultural valence in mainland China than in Western contexts — being called a dog (狗) is itself an insult, making 狗逼 a doubly loaded compound. Less common than 傻逼 or 臭逼 in everyday usage; tends toward maximum-aggression registers."}
{"term":"蠢逼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"chǔn bī","category":"insults","subcategory":"vulva_derived_insult","concept":"cunt_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'dumb/idiotic cunt'; an insult for a profoundly stupid person, synonymous with 傻逼 but using the slightly more formal character 蠢 for stupidity.","english_term":"dumb cunt / fucking moron","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"蠢 (chǔn) means stupid, clumsy, or dull-witted; the distinction from 傻逼 is minimal in practice. Both 蠢 and 傻 freely compound with 逼 as the standard formula for 'stupid person + maximum vulgarity.' Slightly less ubiquitous than 傻逼 but the same structural pattern and force."}
{"term":"怂逼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"sǒng bī","category":"insults","subcategory":"cowardice_vulva_insult","concept":"pussy_coward","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":87,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":86,"recognition_pct_peak":86,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'cowardly cunt'; an insult calling someone a spineless coward by compounding 怂 (timid, easily frightened) with 逼 for maximum vulgarity.","english_term":"pussy / coward","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"怂 (sǒng) means cowardly or easily cowed; the 逼 compound makes it a standard crude insult. Structurally and semantically parallel to English 'pussy' as a cowardice insult, including the use of a female genital term to imply weakness. Common in mainland Chinese internet speech; often censored as 怂B."}
